[Desktop Entry]
Version=1.0
Name=Chromium
GenericName=Web Browser
Comment=Browse the web
Exec=/usr/bin/chromium-browser --ozone-platform=wayland --password-store=basic --enable-features=VaapiVideoDecoder,VaapiVideoDecodeLinuxGL %U
Terminal=false
Icon=chromium
Type=Application
Categories=Network;WebBrowser;
MimeType=text/html;text/xml;application/xhtml+xml;x-scheme-handler/http;x-scheme-handler/https;
StartupNotify=true
Actions=new-window;incognito;

[Desktop Action new-window]
Name=New Window
Exec=/usr/bin/chromium-browser --ozone-platform=wayland --password-store=basic --enable-features=VaapiVideoDecoder,VaapiVideoDecodeLinuxGL

[Desktop Action incognito]
Name=New Incognito Window
Exec=/usr/bin/chromium-browser --ozone-platform=wayland --password-store=basic --enable-features=VaapiVideoDecoder,VaapiVideoDecodeLinuxGL --incognito

# Three flags, each for a reason BishOS creates.
#
# --password-store=basic
#   Chromium keeps the key that encrypts its cookies in the system keyring,
#   reached over D-Bus. There is no D-Bus here, so the key is never stored --
#   and on the next start the cookies cannot be decrypted, are discarded, and
#   every login is gone. History is not encrypted, which is exactly why that
#   survives while the sign-in does not.
#   "basic" uses a fixed key instead, so cookies persist. They are then
#   readable by anyone holding the file, which on an unencrypted USB stick was
#   already true of everything else.
#
# --ozone-platform=wayland
#   Otherwise Chromium runs with --ozone-platform-hint=auto and guesses. It
#   has been guessing right; that is not the same as being told.
#
# --enable-features=VaapiVideoDecoder,VaapiVideoDecodeLinuxGL
#   Hardware video decoding through i965_drv_video.so, the VA-API driver for
#   Haswell. chrome://gpu confirms it took: "Video Decode: Hardware
#   accelerated".
#
#   It buys less than it sounds like. vainfo lists what this GPU can decode --
#   MPEG-2, H.264, VC-1, JPEG -- and that is all. No VP9, no AV1; the silicon
#   is from 2014 and both codecs came after it. YouTube serves VP9 or AV1, so
#   it never reaches the decoder and one core sits near 60% through a 1080p
#   video. That is software VP9 working correctly, not a broken flag, and no
#   configuration fixes it -- only the h264ify extension, which asks YouTube
#   for H.264 instead.
#
#   What the flag does help: local files (mostly H.264, so mpv --hwdec=vaapi
#   decodes on the GPU) and the many sites still serving H.264.
